     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**Yes, financially this is a strong offer for a Google L6 PM in the Bay Area.** \n\nGoogle's typical L6 PM total compensation band centers around **$520,000\u2013$620,000/year**, with $1.08M in new-hire equity sitting in the top quartile of initial grant sizes.\n\nHowever, how the numbers look across different time horizons is an important factor:\n\n---\n\n### 2. Side-by-Side Financial Comparison\n\nGoogle's standard new-hire equity vests on a **front-loaded schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)**:\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Year 1 Equity Vest** | $200,000 | $356,400 (33% of $1.08M) | **+$156,400** |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$476,000** | **~$638,400** | **+$162,400 (+34%)** |\n| **Year 2 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 (run-rate) | ~$638,400 (+ refreshers) | **+$112,400** |\n| **Year 3 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 (run-rate) | ~$520,000 (+ refreshers) | Parity / Dependent on refreshers |\n| **Year 4 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 (run-rate) | ~$412,000 (+ refreshers) | Deficit without refreshers |\n| **4-Yr Average (No Refreshes)** | ~$526,000/yr | **$552,000/yr** | **+$26,000/yr** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **Massive Year 1 & 2 Upside:** Because Google front-loads 66% of equity into the first 24 months, your cash-plus-vest income jumps by over **$160,000 in Year 1** and over **$110,000 in Year 2**.\n2. **The \"Year 3/4 Cliff\":** Starting in Year 3, your initial grant drops to 22%, then 12% in Year 4. Google's annual performance refreshers (which vest linearly over 4 years at 25%/yr) are intended to stack and cushion this drop, but as the recruiter noted, they are performance-dependent.\n3. **Base Salary Step-Down:** Moving from Meta to Google shouldn't involve taking a pay cut on guaranteed base pay, especially since Google's posted range goes up to **$271,000**.\n4. **Missing Sign-On Bonus:** Leaving Meta means forfeiting accrued bonus eligibility and unvested equity momentum without any transition cushion.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should you accept, negotiate, or walk away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou have the strongest possible leverage profile:\n- You were passively sourced (they came to you).\n- You are genuinely happy at Meta and have no urgent reason to leave.\n- You have strong performance and credibility where you are.\n\nGoogle expects candidates at L6/IC6 to negotiate, and doing so will not put the offer at risk as long as you remain collaborative and enthusiastic.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to ask for (and how much)\n\nFocus your negotiation on **Base Salary** and a **Sign-On Bonus**, with a modest stretch on **Equity**.\n\n#### Specific Target Asks:\n1. **Base Salary:** Ask for **$255,000** (target settling around **$245,000\u2013$250,000**).\n   - *Rationale:* Your current base is $240,000, and $255,000 is still safely within their posted $183k\u2013$271k band.\n2. **Sign-On Bonus:** Ask for **$60,000\u2013$75,000**.\n   - *Rationale:* Offsets forfeited near-term bonuses/vests at Meta and adds guaranteed cash upfront.\n3. **Equity (RSUs):** Ask for **$1,200,000** over 4 years (up from $1,080,000).\n   - *Rationale:* Anchors your 4-year annualized run rate higher ($300k/yr average) and cushions the Years 3 and 4 vesting drop.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Email Template\n\nYou can adapt this response to your recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for walking me through the details of the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team throughout the interview process and am excited about the impact of this scope in SF.*\n>\n> *I want to be transparent: because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and compensation at Meta, making a move needs to be a clear step forward across all dimensions.*\n>\n> *Looking at the numbers:*\n> 1. *My current base salary is $240,000, so $235,000 is a step backward on guaranteed cash. Given the posted range for this role goes up to $271,000, I'd like to see the base salary at **$255,000**.*\n> 2. *Leaving Meta mid-cycle means walking away from upcoming bonus accruals and unvested equity. A sign-on bonus of **$70,000** would bridge that gap.*\n> 3. *To ensure competitive compensation through Years 3 and 4 given the front-loaded vesting schedule, I\u2019d like to see the equity grant adjusted to **$1,200,000**.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package closer to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and focus on planning my start date.\"*",
