     "content": "### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n\nGoogle matched your base salary and increased the equity grant by **+$200,000** (to $1.28M total). Because of Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule (**33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**), here is how your compensation looks:\n\n| Component | Meta (Status Quo) | Google Revised Offer | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $0 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | **+$12,000** |\n| **Year 1 Equity Vest** | $200,000 | $422,400 (33% of $1.28M) | **+$222,400** |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$476,000** | **~$710,400** | **+$234,400 (+49%)** |\n| **Year 2 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 | **~$710,400 + refreshers** | **+$184,400+** |\n| **Year 3 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 | **~$569,600 + refreshers** | **+$43,600+** |\n| **Year 4 Total Comp** | ~$526,000 | **~$441,600 + refreshers** | (Refresher dependent) |\n| **4-Year Baseline Average** | **~$526,000/yr** | **~$608,000/yr** | **+$82,000/yr** |\n\n*(Note: Google refresher grants are awarded annually and stack linearly over 4 years at 25%/year, which will lift Years 3 and 4 total compensation significantly above the baseline numbers shown above.)*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Financial Assessment\n\n**From a purely financial standpoint, this is an outstanding offer.**\n\n* **Year 1 & 2 Windfall:** You will earn **~$710,000/year** in your first two years. That represents **~$418,000 in net surplus earnings** over staying at Meta for the next 24 months.\n* **The $200k \"Forfeited\" RSU Myth:** The extra $222k in Year 1 equity alone completely replaces the $200k of Meta equity you forfeit over the next 12 months, and the higher target bonus adds another $12k on top.\n* **Top-of-Band Grant:** A $1.28M initial grant for an L6 PM is at the 90th+ percentile of Google's compensation band for Senior PMs.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Real Decision: Beyond the Compensation\n\nSince the recruiter has explicitly signaled this is their final offer, further negotiation on top-line numbers is unlikely to yield results without risking goodwill. The choice comes down to organizational and career dynamics:\n\n#### Reasons to Accept (Take Google)\n1. **Front-Loaded Wealth Generation:** Bank an extra ~$400k+ in pre-tax cash flow over the next two years.\n2. **Resume Diversification:** Having both Meta (known for execution, rapid ship cycles, and high individual agency) and Google (known for massive scale, matrixed leadership, and platform thinking) on your resume makes you widely marketable for Director/VP (L7/L8) roles anywhere in tech.\n3. **Pace and Sustainable Cadence:** While team-dependent, Google generally offers a more sustainable work-life balance and less volatile operational churn compared to Meta's rigorous bi-annual review and re-org cycles.\n\n#### Reasons to Walk Away (Stay at Meta)\n1. **Product Velocity and Culture:** If you thrive on Meta\u2019s bottoms-up, high-ownership, move-fast culture, Google's consensus-driven, matrixed stakeholder environment can feel slow and process-heavy for PMs.\n2. **Promotion Track (L6 to L7):** Promoting from IC6 to IC7 at Meta is difficult, but promoting from L6 to L7 at Google is notoriously slow and heavily committee-driven. If your near-term goal (next 1\u20132 years) is reaching Director / IC7 level, staying at Meta where you already have established political capital and high performance ratings might get you there faster.\n3. **The Team/Domain:** If the specific Google SF team/product is high-friction, unexciting, or low-priority compared to what you currently own at Meta, the money alone won't insulate you from day-to-day frustration.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommendation\n\n* **Take the offer if:** You want to lock in a massive ~$710k/yr cash flow for the next two years, broaden your Big Tech pedigree, and the specific team/scope in SF interests you.\n* **Stay at Meta only if:** You actively dislike the specific Google product area, or you are on the cusp of an IC7 promotion at Meta within the next 12 months that would fundamentally restructure your baseline compensation and scope.",
